= Implicature =

== Overview ==
An '''implicature''' is content that a speaker conveys beyond what is said in a narrower literal or conventional sense.

The idea is especially associated with H. P. Grice, who argued that hearers can infer additional speaker meaning from what was said, the [[Context (linguistics)|context]], and expectations about cooperative conversation.

== Conversational implicature ==
Suppose someone asks:

<blockquote>Did every student pass?</blockquote>

and the reply is:

<blockquote>Some students passed.</blockquote>

In an appropriate context, the reply may suggest that not all students passed, even though the word ''some'' does not logically entail ''not all''.

That additional interpretation is a classic scalar implicature.

== Cancellability ==
Conversational implicatures are often defeasible:

<blockquote>Some students passed—in fact, all of them did.</blockquote>

The continuation cancels the usual “not all” implication without producing a logical contradiction.

Cancellability is an important diagnostic, not a universal mechanical test for every pragmatic phenomenon.

== Implicating and inferring ==
A speaker '''implicates''' something.

A hearer may '''infer''' it.

These are related but not identical descriptions. A hearer can draw an inference the speaker never intended, and a speaker can attempt to implicate something that a hearer fails to recover.

== Implicature and presupposition ==
[[Presupposition]] typically concerns backgrounded information treated as given or taken for granted.

Implicature concerns additional communicated content.

The distinction is theoretically important even though real cases can be difficult to classify.

== Conventional and conversational accounts ==
Grice distinguished conversational implicatures from some conventional contributions associated with particular expressions.

Later work debates whether various inferences are semantic, conventional, grammatical, pragmatic, probabilistic, or generated through competing alternatives.

